# What:  Canonical contract listing every environment variable RRMS Phase 1
#        consumes, grouped by sensitivity. Two groups:
#        (1) Server-only secrets (no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix) — DATABASE_URL,
#            BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, BETTER_AUTH_URL, the LINE Messaging API
#            triple (channel secret / access token / internal group ID), the
#            LINE Login OAuth pair, the Google OAuth pair, and the Dropbox
#            OAuth triple (app key / app secret / refresh token).
#        (2) Public identifiers (NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix) — NEXT_PUBLIC_LIFF_ID
#            and NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ENV. These ship to the browser bundle and
#            must NEVER hold a secret value.
#        Every key here ends with `=` and no value, except NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ENV
#        which carries the illustrative default `development`.
#
# Why:   Implements spec §6.7.2 機密歸屬清單 (secret-ownership list). This
#        file is the single source of truth that pairs every Phase 1 env var
#        with its sensitivity class, so a new developer cloning the repo runs
#        `cp .env.example .env.local` and knows exactly which slots to fill.
#        Defense-in-depth around the public/secret split: gitleaks (Layer 2
#        pre-commit + Layer 4 CI) detects accidental real secrets, and the
#        ESLint `no-public-secret-vars` rule rejects any source code that
#        reads `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_*` whose name contains SECRET, KEY,
#        TOKEN, or PASSWORD. Together those two guards make it structurally
#        impossible for a secret to leak into the browser bundle via a
#        mis-prefixed env var.
#
# Where: Read by Next.js in three places:
#        - Build time: `next build` inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* values into client
#          bundles. Server-only vars stay out of the client bundle entirely.
#        - Runtime (server): Route Handlers, Server Components, Server
#          Actions, and middleware read `process.env.*` (all 15 vars).
#        - Browser: only the two NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars are visible.
#        On Vercel, the actual values come from Project Settings → Env Vars
#        (Production / Preview / Development scopes), NOT from this file.
#        This file exists only so local developers know what `.env.local`
#        needs to contain.
#
# When:  Touched at developer setup time (initial `cp .env.example .env.local`)
#        and whenever Phase 1 adds, renames, or retires an env var. Vercel
#        reads its own env-var settings on every build and every Function
#        cold start; this file is never deployed to Vercel. `.env.local` is
#        listed in .gitignore (`.env.*` blanket-ignore + explicit `!.env.example`
#        re-include) so real values never reach git history.

# ==== 伺服器機密（無 NEXT_PUBLIC_ 前綴；絕不可洩漏到瀏覽器） ====

# Postgres connection string (Neon, 由 Vercel Marketplace 注入)
DATABASE_URL=
# Direct (unpooled) Postgres connection — used by Drizzle migrations & long
# transactions; Vercel Marketplace Neon integration injects both vars.
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=

# Better Auth
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=

# Vercel Cron authentication secret — verifies cron-job HTTP requests are
# from Vercel and not external. Set in Vercel env (Plan 8 Task 1 Step 2).
CRON_SECRET=

# LINE Messaging API (官方帳號 / OA)
LINE_MESSAGING_CHANNEL_SECRET=
LINE_MESSAGING_CHANNEL_ACCESS_TOKEN=
LINE_INTERNAL_GROUP_ID=

# LINE Login (admin 後台登入)
LINE_LOGIN_CHANNEL_ID=
LINE_LOGIN_CHANNEL_SECRET=

# Google OAuth (admin 後台登入)
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Dropbox (媒體上傳)
DROPBOX_APP_KEY=
DROPBOX_APP_SECRET=
DROPBOX_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# ==== 公開識別碼（可加 NEXT_PUBLIC_ 前綴，會打包到瀏覽器） ====

# LIFF ID (LINE Front-end Framework)
NEXT_PUBLIC_LIFF_ID=

# 環境名稱（顯示用）
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ENV=development
